---Represents a logical validation result from automated reasoning policy evaluation. The finding indicates whether claims in the input are logically valid, invalid, satisfiable, impossible, or have other logical issues.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningFinding
---@field valid? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningValidFinding Contains the result when the automated reasoning evaluation determines that the claims in the input are logically valid and definitively true based on the provided premises and policy rules.
---@field invalid? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningInvalidFinding Contains the result when the automated reasoning evaluation determines that the claims in the input are logically invalid and contradict the established premises or policy rules.
---@field satisfiable? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningSatisfiableFinding Contains the result when the automated reasoning evaluation determines that the claims in the input could be either true or false depending on additional assumptions not provided in the input context.
---@field impossible? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningImpossibleFinding Contains the result when the automated reasoning evaluation determines that no valid logical conclusions can be drawn due to contradictions in the premises or policy rules themselves.
---@field translationAmbiguous? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationAmbiguousFinding Contains the result when the automated reasoning evaluation detects that the input has multiple valid logical interpretations, requiring additional context or clarification to proceed with validation.
---@field tooComplex? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTooComplexFinding Contains the result when the automated reasoning evaluation cannot process the input due to its complexity or volume exceeding the system's processing capacity for logical analysis.
---@field noTranslations? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningNoTranslationsFinding Contains the result when the automated reasoning evaluation cannot extract any relevant logical information from the input that can be validated against the policy rules.

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningFindingList aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningFinding[]

---Indicates that no valid claims can be made due to logical contradictions in the premises or rules.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningImpossibleFinding
---@field translation? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslation The logical translation of the input that this finding evaluates.
---@field contradictingRules? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningRuleList The automated reasoning policy rules that contradict the claims and/or premises in the input.
---@field logicWarning? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningLogicWarning Indication of a logic issue with the translation without needing to consider the automated reasoning policy rules.

---References a portion of the original input text that corresponds to logical elements.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningInputTextReference
---@field text? distilled.Secret The specific text from the original input that this reference points to.

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningInputTextReferenceList aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningInputTextReference[]

---Indicates that the claims are logically false and contradictory to the established rules or premises.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningInvalidFinding
---@field translation? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslation The logical translation of the input that this finding invalidates.
---@field contradictingRules? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningRuleList The automated reasoning policy rules that contradict the claims in the input.
---@field logicWarning? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningLogicWarning Indication of a logic issue with the translation without needing to consider the automated reasoning policy rules.

---Identifies logical issues in the translated statements that exist independent of any policy rules, such as statements that are always true or always false.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningLogicWarning
---@field type? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningLogicWarningType The category of the detected logical issue, such as statements that are always true or always false.
---@field premises? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatementList The logical statements that serve as premises under which the claims are validated.
---@field claims? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatementList The logical statements that are validated while assuming the policy and premises.

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningLogicWarningType "ALWAYS_FALSE"|"ALWAYS_TRUE"

---Indicates that no relevant logical information could be extracted from the input for validation.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningNoTranslationsFinding

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningPoliciesProcessed number

---Contains the results of automated reasoning policy evaluation, including logical findings about the validity of claims made in the input content.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningPolicyAssessment
---@field findings? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningFindingList List of logical validation results produced by evaluating the input content against automated reasoning policies.

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningPolicyUnitsProcessed number

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningPolicyVersionArn string

---References a specific automated reasoning policy rule that was applied during evaluation.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningRule
---@field identifier? aws.bedrock_runtime.AutomatedReasoningRuleIdentifier The unique identifier of the automated reasoning rule.
---@field policyVersionArn? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningPolicyVersionArn The ARN of the automated reasoning policy version that contains this rule.

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningRuleList aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningRule[]

---Indicates that the claims could be either true or false depending on additional assumptions not provided in the input.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningSatisfiableFinding
---@field translation? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslation The logical translation of the input that this finding evaluates.
---@field claimsTrueScenario? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningScenario An example scenario demonstrating how the claims could be logically true.
---@field claimsFalseScenario? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningScenario An example scenario demonstrating how the claims could be logically false.
---@field logicWarning? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningLogicWarning Indication of a logic issue with the translation without needing to consider the automated reasoning policy rules.

---Represents a logical scenario where claims can be evaluated as true or false, containing specific logical assignments.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningScenario
---@field statements? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatementList List of logical assignments and statements that define this scenario.

---A logical statement that includes both formal logic representation and natural language explanation.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatement
---@field logic? distilled.Secret The formal logical representation of the statement.
---@field naturalLanguage? distilled.Secret The natural language explanation of the logical statement.

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatementList aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatement[]

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatementLogicContent string

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatementNaturalLanguageContent string

---Indicates that the input exceeds the processing capacity due to the volume or complexity of the logical information.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTooComplexFinding

---Contains the logical translation of natural language input into formal logical statements, including premises, claims, and confidence scores.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslation
---@field premises? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatementList The logical statements that serve as the foundation or assumptions for the claims.
---@field claims? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningStatementList The logical statements that are being validated against the premises and policy rules.
---@field untranslatedPremises? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningInputTextReferenceList References to portions of the original input text that correspond to the premises but could not be fully translated.
---@field untranslatedClaims? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningInputTextReferenceList References to portions of the original input text that correspond to the claims but could not be fully translated.
---@field confidence? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationConfidence A confidence score between 0 and 1 indicating how certain the system is about the logical translation.

---Indicates that the input has multiple valid logical interpretations, requiring additional context or clarification.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationAmbiguousFinding
---@field options? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationOptionList Different logical interpretations that were detected during translation of the input.
---@field differenceScenarios? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningDifferenceScenarioList Scenarios showing how the different translation options differ in meaning.

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationConfidence number

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationList aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslation[]

---Represents one possible logical interpretation of ambiguous input content.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationOption
---@field translations? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationList Example translations that provide this possible interpretation of the input.

---@alias aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationOptionList aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslationOption[]

---Indicates that the claims are definitively true and logically implied by the premises, with no possible alternative interpretations.
---@class aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningValidFinding
---@field translation? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningTranslation The logical translation of the input that this finding validates.
---@field claimsTrueScenario? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningScenario An example scenario demonstrating how the claims are logically true.
---@field supportingRules? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningRuleList The automated reasoning policy rules that support why this result is considered valid.
---@field logicWarning? aws.bedrock_runtime.GuardrailAutomatedReasoningLogicWarning Indication of a logic issue with the translation without needing to consider the automated reasoning policy rules.

